const reconstruction = `SELECT l.tenant_id, l.booking_id, l.service_id,
       s.service_name, c.category_label, l.quantity
FROM booked_services AS l
JOIN services AS s USING (tenant_id, service_id)
JOIN service_categories AS c USING (tenant_id, category_id)
ORDER BY l.tenant_id, l.booking_id, l.service_id;`;
const total = `SELECT b.tenant_id, b.booking_id,
       CASE WHEN b.status = 'booked' THEN
         coalesce(sum(l.quantity * l.agreed_unit_fee_eur)
           FILTER (WHERE l.status = 'active'), 0)
         ELSE 0 END AS optional_subtotal_eur
FROM bookings AS b
LEFT JOIN booked_services AS l USING (tenant_id, booking_id)
GROUP BY b.tenant_id, b.booking_id, b.status;`;
---

<p><strong>Normalisation</strong> gives each fact a place determined by what it describes. If a service’s name appears on twenty booked lines, correcting that name means finding twenty copies. Giving the service its own row makes the correction one change. The booked lines keep their quantities and their references to that service.</p>
<p>The difficult part is deciding which facts can be separated without changing their meaning. A split can forget who booked what. It can also preserve every relationship while making an important rule harder to enforce. Keeping a calculated result introduces a different obligation: keeping that copy in step with its source.</p>
<p>We will extend the class-booking service from <a href="/review/relational-next/data-design/#store-each-fact-where-it-belongs">Designing relational data</a>. Mina’s B11 books session S21. Optional services now include equipment hire and refreshments. The parent already separates current offers from agreed fees: those are different facts, as <a href="/review/relational-next/history/">history modelling</a> develops. Here we follow dependencies, reconstruction and maintenance of derived copies.</p>

